1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:05,687
Most of an application's documentation is spread across many different files.

2
00:00:06,137 --> 00:00:11,605
The requirement lives in a spreadsheet, the coding standard in a Markdown file,

3
00:00:11,605 --> 00:00:16,588
the API in its specification, the test results in a traceability matrix,

4
00:00:16,588 --> 00:00:18,803
and the outage in a Word report.

5
00:00:19,253 --> 00:00:24,274
Each file holds one piece of the story, but none of them can tell it alone.

6
00:00:24,724 --> 00:00:29,184
So nobody puts the pieces together, until one afternoon in August,

7
00:00:29,184 --> 00:00:32,225
a single search takes the whole service down.

8
00:00:33,542 --> 00:00:38,722
Let's see how Digital Brain, a Python platform, brings that story together,

9
00:00:38,722 --> 00:00:44,455
starting with just one of these files: the non-functional requirements spreadsheet.

10
00:00:44,905 --> 00:00:47,230
As soon as the file is uploaded,

11
00:00:47,230 --> 00:00:53,330
the platform performs a security check and takes a unique SHA-256 fingerprint of it,

12
00:00:53,330 --> 00:00:56,381
so we can always tell if it changes later.

13
00:00:57,667 --> 00:01:03,789
Next, MarkItDown, an open-source converter, turns the spreadsheet into Markdown:

14
00:01:03,789 --> 00:01:07,462
plain text that both people and an LLM can read.

15
00:01:07,912 --> 00:01:12,452
Each sheet becomes a heading, and each row becomes a line in a table.

16
00:01:12,902 --> 00:01:15,339
Now look at this highlighted row.

17
00:01:15,619 --> 00:01:19,555
It says something simple: when someone asks for a list,

18
00:01:19,555 --> 00:01:23,206
the server must limit how many items it sends back.

19
00:01:23,656 --> 00:01:25,715
We'll call it the page-size rule,

20
00:01:25,715 --> 00:01:30,020
and the rest of this story is about what happened when it was broken.

21
00:01:30,470 --> 00:01:33,623
The Markdown also gets a fingerprint of its own,

22
00:01:33,623 --> 00:01:38,155
because every answer will later be checked against exactly this text.

23
00:01:39,458 --> 00:01:45,195
Before building anything, there is one more question to ask: can we trust this file?

24
00:01:45,645 --> 00:01:48,999
A graph is only as good as what goes into it.

25
00:01:49,279 --> 00:01:55,037
A table with no column names gives you nodes called "column one" and "column two",

26
00:01:55,037 --> 00:02:00,092
a half-finished document marked TBD gives answers that are not true yet,

27
00:02:00,092 --> 00:02:03,463
and a badly scanned page, read by Tesseract OCR,

28
00:02:03,463 --> 00:02:06,623
can match a quote exactly and still be wrong.

29
00:02:07,073 --> 00:02:10,790
So the platform scores every document out of hundred,

30
00:02:10,790 --> 00:02:14,017
using six rule-based checks written in Python,

31
00:02:14,017 --> 00:02:18,646
with no LLM involved, so the same file always gets the same score.

32
00:02:19,096 --> 00:02:21,790
Our spreadsheet scores a full hundred.

33
00:02:22,070 --> 00:02:25,732
A raw export with no column names scores only sixty,

34
00:02:25,732 --> 00:02:29,957
and the platform shows exactly which points it lost and why.

35
00:02:30,407 --> 00:02:36,740
The score is advice, not a gate: the owner decides whether to fix the file or go ahead.

36
00:02:38,042 --> 00:02:43,569
Now Python code builds the first graph, and it uses just four kinds of node.

37
00:02:44,019 --> 00:02:47,250
The whole spreadsheet becomes a document node.

38
00:02:47,530 --> 00:02:50,837
Each heading inside it becomes a section node,

39
00:02:50,837 --> 00:02:53,999
like Availability, Performance and Security.

40
00:02:54,449 --> 00:02:59,274
Each table row becomes a record node, and here is our page-size rule.

41
00:02:59,724 --> 00:03:02,772
And each cell in that row becomes a value node:

42
00:03:02,772 --> 00:03:06,468
the rule is checked by code review and an automated test,

43
00:03:06,468 --> 00:03:09,321
and if it is broken, the release is blocked.

44
00:03:09,771 --> 00:03:14,897
Three other rules also block the release, so they share this same value node,

45
00:03:14,897 --> 00:03:17,826
and that is how related rules get connected.

46
00:03:18,276 --> 00:03:21,537
Every node keeps the exact line it came from,

47
00:03:21,537 --> 00:03:26,247
and since no LLM has been used so far, all of this costs nothing.

48
00:03:27,542 --> 00:03:31,731
Now let's add the other four files: the coding standard,

49
00:03:31,731 --> 00:03:37,566
the API specification, the test traceability matrix and the outage postmortem.

50
00:03:38,016 --> 00:03:42,751
Each one goes through the same steps, and all four score a full hundred.

51
00:03:43,201 --> 00:03:47,908
Looking across the files, the source check notices something interesting:

52
00:03:47,908 --> 00:03:50,939
three of them point to the same page-size rule.

53
00:03:51,389 --> 00:03:56,270
And when we read them together, the story they tell is quite insightful.

54
00:03:56,720 --> 00:04:02,602
In May, the test review found that patient search had no upper limit on its page size.

55
00:04:03,052 --> 00:04:07,113
In June, that finding was waived so the release could go ahead,

56
00:04:07,113 --> 00:04:10,077
bundled with two others under a single reason.

57
00:04:10,527 --> 00:04:15,640
Then in August, someone searched patients with a limit of one lakh records.

58
00:04:16,090 --> 00:04:18,991
The service tried to read the whole table,

59
00:04:18,991 --> 00:04:23,481
and for forty-five minutes, sixty percent of all requests failed.

60
00:04:24,792 --> 00:04:29,317
We could see that story because we read all five files ourselves.

61
00:04:29,767 --> 00:04:33,525
The graph cannot, yet: it links things inside each file,

62
00:04:33,525 --> 00:04:36,275
but it cannot tell that the outage in the

63
00:04:36,275 --> 00:04:39,563
Word report is about the rule in the spreadsheet.

64
00:04:40,013 --> 00:04:45,474
To connect the files by meaning, we bring in an LLM, OpenAI or Claude.

65
00:04:45,924 --> 00:04:51,917
It reads each file, but it may only use a short, fixed list: eight kinds of thing,

66
00:04:51,917 --> 00:04:58,201
like a requirement or an incident, and twelve kinds of link, like affects or violates.

67
00:04:58,651 --> 00:05:03,286
To keep costs low, it reads only files that are new or have changed,

68
00:05:03,286 --> 00:05:05,944
and it stays within the monthly budget.

69
00:05:06,394 --> 00:05:12,311
So it suggests links like these: the August outage affected the patient search API,

70
00:05:12,311 --> 00:05:15,019
and that API broke the page-size rule.

71
00:05:15,469 --> 00:05:18,298
The same standard of quality applies here:

72
00:05:18,298 --> 00:05:22,607
the LLM must prove every link with an exact quote from the file,

73
00:05:22,607 --> 00:05:25,705
and only links with a matching quote are kept.

74
00:05:26,155 --> 00:05:32,128
If the quote is not in the file word for word, the link is thrown away, like this one.

75
00:05:32,578 --> 00:05:38,577
And when two files name the same API in the same way, they are joined into one node.

76
00:05:39,027 --> 00:05:43,274
Now the outage, the API and the rule form one connected chain,

77
00:05:43,274 --> 00:05:48,959
so a question about the outage can be traced all the way back to the rule it broke.

78
00:05:50,250 --> 00:05:55,755
Before anyone asks a question, the platform gets ready to search in two ways.

79
00:05:56,205 --> 00:06:02,913
A BM25 keyword index finds passages by their exact words, which is fast and free.

80
00:06:03,363 --> 00:06:07,948
If the owner turns on embeddings, each passage also gets a vector,

81
00:06:07,948 --> 00:06:10,657
so a question can match by meaning too.

82
00:06:11,107 --> 00:06:16,531
Then, from the best matches, the search follows the graph's links two steps further,

83
00:06:16,531 --> 00:06:21,373
which is how a question about the outage can reach the rule and the waiver.

84
00:06:22,667 --> 00:06:24,905
Every build is saved as a draft,

85
00:06:24,905 --> 00:06:29,592
and Graphify groups its nodes into themes so people can explore it.

86
00:06:30,042 --> 00:06:33,476
Before anyone relies on it, the draft is tested:

87
00:06:33,476 --> 00:06:39,199
on CarePath's sixteen test questions, the right document comes back for fifteen.

88
00:06:39,649 --> 00:06:42,485
Once a person reviews it and publishes it,

89
00:06:42,485 --> 00:06:45,592
only that published version answers questions.

90
00:06:46,042 --> 00:06:51,045
So let's ask: why did the service go down on the fourteenth of August?

91
00:06:51,495 --> 00:06:56,768
The answer points to the postmortem, the test finding and the page-size rule,

92
00:06:56,768 --> 00:07:00,808
and each source is checked again, live, before it is shown.

93
00:07:01,258 --> 00:07:03,470
The pieces were always there.

94
00:07:03,750 --> 00:07:08,954
Now, for the first time, they tell the story together: the rule, the gap,

95
00:07:08,954 --> 00:07:13,944
the waiver and the outage, each pointing back to where it was written.
